1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:13,309
In the early hours of the twenty-seventh of May 1991, two young women in Milwaukee found a boy in the street. He was fourteen, naked, dazed and barely able to stand.

2
00:00:14,409 --> 00:00:24,500
They called the police. When officers arrived, a calm, fair-haired man of thirty-one told them the boy was his adult boyfriend, drunk after a quarrel.

3
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:34,645
The officers walked the boy back to the man's flat and left. His name was Konerak Sinthasomphone. He was killed shortly after they had gone.

4
00:00:35,745 --> 00:00:45,473
The man was Jeffrey Dahmer. Between 1978 and 1991, he killed seventeen boys and young men.

5
00:00:46,573 --> 00:00:54,829
This film is not about his mind. It is about the people he took, and the many moments when someone could have stopped him, and did not.

6
00:00:57,029 --> 00:01:10,427
Jeffrey Dahmer was born in Milwaukee on the twenty-first of May 1960. His father, Lionel, was a chemist, and when Jeffrey was a young boy the family moved to rural Ohio.

7
00:01:11,527 --> 00:01:19,185
He was a quiet, isolated boy. By high school he was drinking heavily, and his parents' marriage was falling apart.

8
00:01:20,285 --> 00:01:32,403
In June 1978, weeks after finishing school, he picked up an eighteen-year-old hitchhiker named Steven Hicks, who was on his way to a rock concert, and killed him.

9
00:01:33,503 --> 00:01:40,265
Steven's family never knew what had happened to him. For thirteen years he was simply a missing person.

10
00:01:41,365 --> 00:01:52,629
Dahmer dropped out of university after one term. His father pushed him into the army, which discharged him early, in 1981, because of his drinking.

11
00:01:54,829 --> 00:02:09,315
He was sent to live with his grandmother in West Allis, just outside Milwaukee. He was arrested more than once in those years, for drunkenness and for exposing himself, but each time it was treated as a minor offence.

12
00:02:10,415 --> 00:02:20,569
In late 1987 he began killing again. Steven Tuomi. James Doxtator, who was just fourteen. Richard Guerrero.

13
00:02:21,669 --> 00:02:37,840
In September 1988, he was arrested for sexually assaulting a thirteen-year-old boy. He was convicted, but sentenced to one year in a work-release jail, which let him leave each day for his job, and five years' probation.

14
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:46,087
While he was waiting to be sentenced, he killed Anthony Sears. No one connected him to the disappearance.

15
00:02:47,187 --> 00:02:55,976
He was released after ten months. His record now said, plainly, that he was a convicted child sex offender. It would matter later.

16
00:02:58,176 --> 00:03:09,675
In May 1990 he moved into a small flat in the Oxford Apartments, on Milwaukee's west side. Over the next fourteen months, he killed twelve more men there.

17
00:03:10,775 --> 00:03:18,497
Most of his victims were young Black, Asian or Latino men. Many were gay or bisexual, and many were poor.

18
00:03:19,597 --> 00:03:28,365
He met most of them in bars, at a shopping centre or on the street, and lured them back to his flat with an offer of money or a drink.

19
00:03:29,465 --> 00:03:38,767
Their families reported them missing. Some later said that the police had shown little interest in young men like their sons and brothers.

20
00:03:39,867 --> 00:03:49,765
Neighbours complained to the building manager about a terrible smell from his flat. He blamed spoiled meat and a broken freezer, and nobody looked further.

21
00:03:51,965 --> 00:04:03,933
Go back to that night in May. The two young women were cousins, Sandra Smith and Nicole Childress, both eighteen. When Dahmer came out to collect the boy, they tried to keep him away.

22
00:04:05,033 --> 00:04:15,423
Three police officers arrived. The women told them the boy was a child and that he was hurt. According to the women, the officers told them to stay out of it.

23
00:04:16,523 --> 00:04:26,336
The officers did not check the boy's age. They did not run Dahmer's name, which would have shown he was on probation for a sexual assault on a child.

24
00:04:27,436 --> 00:04:35,201
They stepped briefly into the flat, but did not look in the bedroom, where the body of an earlier victim, Tony Hughes, lay.

25
00:04:36,301 --> 00:04:45,709
Afterwards, on the police radio, one officer reported that the boy had been returned to his boyfriend. The officers could be heard laughing.

26
00:04:46,809 --> 00:04:57,817
Sandra's mother, Glenda Cleveland, lived in the building next door. She called the police to ask whether they were sure the boy was an adult. An officer assured her that he was.

27
00:04:58,917 --> 00:05:06,768
There was one more terrible detail. Konerak was the younger brother of the boy Dahmer had been convicted of assaulting.

28
00:05:07,868 --> 00:05:16,551
In the eight weeks that followed, Dahmer killed four more men: Matt Turner, Jeremiah Weinberger, Oliver Lacy and Joseph Bradehoft.

29
00:05:18,751 --> 00:05:30,911
On the night of the twenty-second of July 1991, a man named Tracy Edwards escaped from the flat, with a handcuff hanging from one wrist, and flagged down a patrol car.

30
00:05:32,011 --> 00:05:45,408
This time, the two officers, Robert Rauth and Rolf Mueller, went back with him and looked around. Within minutes they found evidence that made the truth impossible to deny. Dahmer was arrested on the spot.

31
00:05:46,508 --> 00:05:55,340
He confessed to police in detail. Over the following weeks, investigators worked to identify each victim and tell their families.

32
00:05:57,540 --> 00:06:10,852
He was charged with fifteen murders in Wisconsin. He pleaded guilty, but argued that he was insane. On the fifteenth of February 1992, a jury rejected that, and found him sane.

33
00:06:11,952 --> 00:06:22,661
Wisconsin has no death penalty. He was sentenced to fifteen consecutive life terms, and later a sixteenth in Ohio, for the killing of Steven Hicks.

34
00:06:23,761 --> 00:06:35,153
At the sentencing, the victims' families spoke. Rita Isbell, the sister of Errol Lindsey, confronted him in a moment of raw grief that was broadcast across the country.

35
00:06:36,253 --> 00:06:43,464
He was sent to Columbia Correctional Institution, a maximum-security prison in Portage, Wisconsin.

36
00:06:44,564 --> 00:06:55,401
On the twenty-eighth of November 1994, while on a cleaning duty, he was beaten to death by another inmate, Christopher Scarver. He was thirty-four.

37
00:06:57,601 --> 00:07:07,969
After the arrest, the city learned what had happened that night in May. Two of the officers who had returned Konerak, John Balcerzak and Joseph Gabrish, were fired.

38
00:07:09,069 --> 00:07:17,475
Both appealed, and both later won their jobs back. Balcerzak went on to become president of the city's police union.

39
00:07:18,575 --> 00:07:34,447
To many in Milwaukee's Black, Asian and gay communities, the case proved what they had been saying for years: that the police did not take them seriously. The mayor set up a commission to examine relations between the police and the city they served.

40
00:07:36,647 --> 00:07:44,284
The Oxford Apartments were demolished in 1992. The site has stood empty ever since.

41
00:07:45,384 --> 00:08:00,744
His name has never gone away. In 2022, a hugely popular television drama retold the story. Some victims' relatives said no one had asked them, and that it made them relive the worst days of their lives.

42
00:08:01,844 --> 00:08:12,703
Dahmer was not a criminal mastermind. He was caught because one man ran. He was missed because, time after time, the people whose job it was to listen did not.

43
00:08:13,803 --> 00:08:25,920
These are the people he killed. Steven Hicks, Steven Tuomi, James Doxtator, Richard Guerrero, Anthony Sears, Raymond Smith, Edward Smith, Ernest Miller and David Thomas.

44
00:08:27,020 --> 00:08:39,308
Curtis Straughter, Errol Lindsey, Tony Hughes, Konerak Sinthasomphone, Matt Turner, Jeremiah Weinberger, Oliver Lacy and Joseph Bradehoft. Theirs are the names worth remembering.
